<p class="Heading3">Ja 58 The Story about the Three Things<br>(Tayodhammajātaka)</p>

<p class="summary">In the present Devadatta sets out to kill the Buddha, who replies that he did this in the past also, and tells a story of how, when he was a monkey, he outwitted his father; and the Rakkhasa who was meant to kill him became his handiman. Terrified by this, his father passed away and he became the new king (<a href="../Jataka/058.htm">full story</a>).</p>

<p class="verse3">1. Yassa ete tayo dhammā, vānarinda yathā tava:<br>
Dakkhiyaṁ sūriyaṁ paññā, diṭṭhaṁ so ativattatī ti.</p>

<p class="translation">He who, monkey-king, like you, has these three things: dexterity, heroism, wisdom, will overcome his foe.</p>

<p>In this connection, <i>dexterity</i> means being adroit, having the knowledge and wisdom to destroy the fear that is present, this is known as being associated with supreme effort.</p>

<p><i>Heroism</i> means being heroic, this is known as the development of fearlessness.</p>

<p><i>Wisdom</i> means this is known as the wisdom in inference, <span class="TT"><span><cite>Padaṭṭhāna</cite>, lit: <em>wisdom in footprints</em>, metaphorically it means <em>inference</em>. </span><sup class="autonumber"></sup></span> wisdom in means.</p>

<p>&#160;</p>

